"""The table's codes for these instances are not at a local optimum of the largest cosine. Local search from the
published coordinates lowered the cosine of 23 of the 28 by more than 1e-12 and of 13 by more than 1e-6, by up to
1.4e-04 (24_116); 23 of them beat the table's displayed (rounded-up) cosine by at least 1e-12. The search per
instance, about eight minutes: sequential linear programming on the near-active pairs (sph.maximize), L-BFGS on the
smooth maximum of the cosines at sharpness rising to 1e9 (sph.descend), and two passes of basin hopping
(sph.improve: shake all points by 1e-4 to 1e-2 of the minimal chord, descend, maximize, keep drops). Where a further
minute of descend and maximize still lowered the cosine by more than 1e-7 (11 of the 28), one-minute passes followed
while they gained more than 1e-8, up to four, up to twelve more on the 2 still moving after those; the longest
instance took 16 minutes. It does not converge: a further minute of descend and maximize still lowers the cosine by
up to 8e-08 on 23 of the 28. Instances where nothing beat the published code keep it. These codes are the bar the
task scores against. COS2 is the exact signed squared cosine of the binary64 points as numerator/denominator, COORDS
the points as base64 of little-endian binary64 rows. SCiEL run sp34a1 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 22 more, which
replace those."""

